@page "/drawer"

<CatHead Title="Drawers and sheets">
    A panel that comes in from an edge, for a secondary flow that should not lose the page behind
    it — a filter panel, a record's detail, a form on a phone.
</CatHead>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-question-line"></i>
    <strong>Drawer or modal?</strong> A <a href="/modal">modal</a> demands an answer before anything
    else happens; a drawer is a place to work that can be left. If dismissing it loses nothing, it is
    a drawer. The drawer sits <em>below</em> the modal backdrop in the z-order, so a modal opened
    from inside a drawer still covers it.
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Drawer.Drawer" Title="From each edge" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — open one.</strong> Each is a <code>&lt;dialog class="drawer"&gt;</code> opened
        with <code>showModal()</code>, so it slides in from its edge, <kbd>Escape</kbd> closes it,
        focus stays inside it, and <code>::backdrop</code> dims the page with no
        <code>.drawer-scrim</code> in the markup at all.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.drawer--start</code> comes from the other edge. <code>.sheet</code> comes from the
        bottom — the right shape on a phone, where a side panel has nowhere to come from.
        <code>.sheet-handle</code> sits inside the header's own top padding rather than above it,
        so a sheet carries one band of chrome and not two.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>A sheet is capped and centred</strong> at <code>min(480px, 100vw)</code>, the way a
        drawer is capped at 420px. One rule serves both ends: on a phone the viewport is the smaller
        value and the sheet is edge to edge with its rounded corners where they belong, on a desktop
        it is 480px with auto margins. A sheet spanning a 1440px window puts those corners at the
        far edges of the screen and its content in a column on the left, which reads as a bar across
        the bottom rather than a panel.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Add <code>data-sheet</code> and the handle does what it looks like it does</strong> —
        drag it or the header down and the sheet follows the pointer, past a quarter of its height
        or with a flick and it closes, short of that it springs back. It is opt-in for the reason
        <code>data-tabs</code> is: an app with its own gesture would otherwise have two things
        closing one dialog. <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> only, because the
        <code>div</code> form's open state is a class the app owns and Blazor would rewrite it back
        on the next render.
    </p>
    <p>
        The handle is still not a control — it takes no focus and carries no name, so the drag is
        deliberately unreachable from the keyboard. <strong>The close button is what makes a sheet
        dismissible</strong>; this is an addition on top of it and never the only way out.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.sheet--dragging</code> is on the sheet while a drag is in flight. The script puts it
        there and takes it away; nothing writes it by hand. It carries the grabbing cursor, and it is
        a hook for an app that wants to do something else for the duration.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.drawer-body</code> is the only scrolling part, so the header and footer stay put. The
        slide survives <code>display: none</code> through
        <code>transition-behavior: allow-discrete</code> and <code>@@starting-style</code>; without
        them the panel appears rather than slides, which is abrupt rather than broken.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Drawer.RecordDetail" Title="A record's detail" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — open it.</strong> The commonest use: the row stays on screen behind the panel,
        so the reader keeps their place in the list. The header carries the
        <a href="/avatar"><code>.list-row</code></a> lockup with <code>--flush</code>, because the
        header already supplies the padding.
    </p>
    <p>
        Everything that scrolls goes in <code>.drawer-body</code>. Point
        <code>aria-labelledby</code> at the title, not at the panel: the title is what a screen reader
        announces when focus enters.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Drawer.ActionSheet" Title="A sheet of actions" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — open it.</strong> A sheet whose body is a <a href="/collections">list</a> of
        actions, which is the phone form of a <a href="/menu">menu</a>. Each row is a
        <code>&lt;button class="list-row"&gt;</code> filling the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>, so the
        whole row is the target and the count is announced. The divider between rows is
        <code>.list</code>'s own.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>There is no separate class for a sheet row.</strong>
        <a href="/collections"><code>.list-row</code></a> resets the platform's button chrome and
        carries hover, pressed, focus and disabled, so an action row and a navigating row are the
        same class on a different element. The pressed state matters more than the hover one here:
        a finger never enters a hover.
    </p>
    <p>
        The body is <code>.drawer-body--flush</code>, the sibling of
        <a href="/card"><code>.card-body--flush</code></a>: the rows meet the sheet's edges, so
        the sheet is the surface they sit on rather than a card inside a padded one. A row in a
        flush body takes the header's own inline inset, which lines the icon column up under the
        title and runs each row's hover fill the full width of the sheet — inside the padding it
        looked clipped at its start edge.
    </p>
    <p>
        Use a sheet rather than a menu when the actions need a second line each, or when the reader is
        on a touch screen where a 36px menu row is a small target.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Drawer/DrawerScrimForm.html" Title="A drawer that is not modal">
    <p>
        <code>.drawer-scrim</code> and <code>.drawer-scrim--open</code> are for a drawer the reader
        can deliberately leave open while working on the page behind it. Closed state is
        <code>visibility: hidden</code>, not just a translate off-screen: a panel that is merely moved
        away is still tabbable, and <kbd>Tab</kbd> walks the reader into a panel they cannot see.
    </p>
    <p>
        A drawer <em>with</em> a scrim is modal in behaviour, so make it a
        <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> instead — the scrim becomes <code>::backdrop</code>, and the
        dismissal, the focus trap and <kbd>Escape</kbd> stop being yours to write.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>
